<h3>How a recipe gets published</h3>
<p>This is the request you will build in Steps 5 to 10. Notice that each tool does a different job: some work happens inside the request, some right after it, and some in a separate process.</p>
<pre><code class="language-plaintext">POST /recipes
 |- CSRF check (global middleware), then the "auth" route middleware
 |- Validator            -&gt; are the form fields acceptable?
 |- FileUploader         -&gt; store the photo (checked by real MIME type)
 |- Doctrine             -&gt; persist() + flush() the Recipe entity
 |- EventDispatcher      -&gt; RecipePublished: clear caches, write a log line   (in the request)
 |- defer()              -&gt; rebuild the JSON export                           (after the response)
 |- QueueManager         -&gt; EmailRecipeConfirmation                           (a worker process, retried)
 '- redirect + flash      -&gt; GET /recipes/{slug}</code></pre>

<hr>

<h2 id="prep">Before You Start</h2>
<p>You need a working Rhapsody application (see <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/installation">Installation &amp; Setup</a>) with <code>APP_URL</code> and your database settings in <code>.env</code>. Then run these commands once. Each one is explained in the step that uses it.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># Users table, so recipes have authors (skip if you already ran it)
php rhapsody auth:install

# React bridge for the islands in Step 11 (skip if you already ran it)
php rhapsody react:install
npm install

# Tables for the persistent queue used in Step 10
php rhapsody queue:install --migrate</code></pre>

<div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4 my-4"><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep <code>APP_URL</code> set even on a local install. <code>redirect()</code> and <code>route()</code> both build addresses from <code>APP_URL</code> and <code>APP_BASE_URL</code>, and a few lines in this tutorial rely on that.</div>

