<h2 id="config">Step 1: Configuration</h2>
<p>Put tunable values in configuration, not in controller code. Read them from the environment with <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/support"><code>Env</code></a> inside <code>config/config.php</code>, where it is allowed to touch the environment, and read them everywhere else through the typed <code>Config</code> service.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">config/config.php (add this key to the array the file returns)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">'recipes' =&gt; [
    'per_page'       =&gt; Env::int('RECIPES_PER_PAGE', 9),
    'export_enabled' =&gt; Env::bool('RECIPES_EXPORT', true),
],</code></pre>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">.env</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">RECIPES_PER_PAGE=9
RECIPES_EXPORT=true</code></pre>
<p>Later, <code>$this->config->int('recipes.per_page', 9)</code> returns a real <code>int</code>, using dot notation to reach the nested key. If a key is missing and you gave no default, <code>Config</code> throws immediately instead of letting a <code>null</code> travel through your code.</p>

<hr>

<h2 id="database">Step 2: The Database Table</h2>
<p>Rhapsody uses Phinx for migrations, so the same file works on MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server and SQLite. Generate the file, then fill in <code>change()</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody make:migration CreateRecipesTable</code></pre>
<p>Open the new file in <code>db/migrations/</code> and replace its body:</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">db/migrations/20261106120000_create_recipes_table.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

use Phinx\Migration\AbstractMigration;

final class CreateRecipesTable extends AbstractMigration
{
    public function change(): void
    {
        $this-&gt;table('recipes')
            -&gt;addColumn('author_id', 'string', ['limit' =&gt; 255])
            -&gt;addColumn('title', 'string', ['limit' =&gt; 160])
            -&gt;addColumn('slug', 'string', ['limit' =&gt; 190])
            -&gt;addColumn('summary', 'string', ['limit' =&gt; 300])
            -&gt;addColumn('ingredients', 'text')
            -&gt;addColumn('steps', 'text')
            -&gt;addColumn('servings', 'integer', ['default' =&gt; 4])
            -&gt;addColumn('photo', 'string', ['limit' =&gt; 100, 'null' =&gt; true])
            -&gt;addColumn('views', 'integer', ['default' =&gt; 0])
            -&gt;addColumn('created_at', 'timestamp', ['default' =&gt; 'CURRENT_TIMESTAMP'])
            -&gt;addIndex(['slug'], ['unique' =&gt; true])
            -&gt;addIndex(['author_id'])
            -&gt;create();
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>Your file name will carry its own timestamp. Now create the table:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody migrate</code></pre>
<p><code>author_id</code> holds the <code>user_id</code> of the built-in <code>users</code> table. We use a plain column rather than a database foreign key to keep the tutorial focused, but adding one is a good exercise.</p>

<hr>

<h2 id="entity">Step 3: The Entity (Writes)</h2>
<p>Use a Doctrine entity when you create or change data. The <code>EntityManager</code> tracks the object and writes only what changed. Entities live in <code>app/Entities/</code>, which the framework scans automatically.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Entities/Recipe.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Entities;

use DateTimeImmutable;
use Doctrine\ORM\Mapping as ORM;

#[ORM\Entity]
#[ORM\Table(name: 'recipes')]
class Recipe
{
    #[ORM\Id]
    #[ORM\GeneratedValue]
    #[ORM\Column(type: 'integer')]
    private ?int $id = null;

    #[ORM\Column(name: 'author_id', type: 'string', length: 255)]
    private string $authorId;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'string', length: 160)]
    private string $title;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'string', length: 190, unique: true)]
    private string $slug;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'string', length: 300)]
    private string $summary;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'text')]
    private string $ingredients;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'text')]
    private string $steps;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'integer')]
    private int $servings;

    #[ORM\Column(type: 'string', length: 100, nullable: true)]
    private ?string $photo;

    #[ORM\Column(name: 'created_at', type: 'datetime_immutable')]
    private DateTimeImmutable $createdAt;

    public function __construct(
        string $authorId,
        string $title,
        string $slug,
        string $summary,
        string $ingredients,
        string $steps,
        int $servings,
        ?string $photo = null,
    ) {
        $this-&gt;authorId    = $authorId;
        $this-&gt;title       = $title;
        $this-&gt;slug        = $slug;
        $this-&gt;summary     = $summary;
        $this-&gt;ingredients = $ingredients;
        $this-&gt;steps       = $steps;
        $this-&gt;servings    = $servings;
        $this-&gt;photo       = $photo;
        $this-&gt;createdAt   = new DateTimeImmutable();
    }

    public function getId(): ?int
    {
        return $this-&gt;id;
    }

    public function getAuthorId(): string
    {
        return $this-&gt;authorId;
    }

    public function getTitle(): string
    {
        return $this-&gt;title;
    }

    public function getSlug(): string
    {
        return $this-&gt;slug;
    }

    public function getPhoto(): ?string
    {
        return $this-&gt;photo;
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>The <code>views</code> column from the migration is deliberately <em>not</em> mapped. Doctrine leaves unmapped columns alone, so a new row simply gets the database default of <code>0</code>, and the view counter in Step 9 can update it with one cheap query instead of loading the whole entity.</p>

<hr>

<h2 id="model">Step 4: A Read Model (Reads)</h2>
<p>Pages that mostly <em>read</em> data, such as lists, sidebars and counters, are a good fit for a plain PDO model: one explicit query, no object hydration. Generate it, then replace the contents.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody make:model RecipeCatalog</code></pre>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Models/RecipeCatalog.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Models;

use PDO;
use Rhapsody\Core\BaseModel;

/**
 * @phpstan-type RecipeRow array{id: int|string, title: string, slug: string, summary: string, photo: string|null, servings: int|string, views: int|string, created_at: string, author_name: string}
 * @phpstan-type RecipeDetail array{id: int|string, author_id: string, title: string, slug: string, summary: string, ingredients: string, steps: string, servings: int|string, photo: string|null, views: int|string, created_at: string, author_name: string}
 */
final class RecipeCatalog extends BaseModel
{
    private const string CARD_COLUMNS = 'r.id, r.title, r.slug, r.summary, r.photo, r.servings, r.views, r.created_at, u.name AS author_name';

    public function countAll(): int
    {
        $stmt = $this-&gt;db-&gt;prepare('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM recipes');
        $stmt-&gt;execute();
        $count = $stmt-&gt;fetchColumn();

        return is_numeric($count) ? (int) $count : 0;
    }

    /**
     * @return list&lt;RecipeRow&gt;
     */
    public function findPaginated(int $limit, int $offset): array
    {
        $stmt = $this-&gt;db-&gt;prepare(
            'SELECT ' . self::CARD_COLUMNS . '
               FROM recipes r
               JOIN users u ON u.user_id = r.author_id
              ORDER BY r.created_at DESC, r.id DESC
              LIMIT :limit OFFSET :offset'
        );
        $stmt-&gt;bindValue(':limit', $limit, PDO::PARAM_INT);
        $stmt-&gt;bindValue(':offset', $offset, PDO::PARAM_INT);
        $stmt-&gt;execute();

        /** @var list&lt;RecipeRow&gt; $rows */
        $rows = $stmt-&gt;fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);

        return $rows;
    }

    /**
     * @return list&lt;RecipeRow&gt;
     */
    public function popular(int $limit): array
    {
        $stmt = $this-&gt;db-&gt;prepare(
            'SELECT ' . self::CARD_COLUMNS . '
               FROM recipes r
               JOIN users u ON u.user_id = r.author_id
              ORDER BY r.views DESC, r.id DESC
              LIMIT :limit'
        );
        $stmt-&gt;bindValue(':limit', $limit, PDO::PARAM_INT);
        $stmt-&gt;execute();

        /** @var list&lt;RecipeRow&gt; $rows */
        $rows = $stmt-&gt;fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);

        return $rows;
    }

    /**
     * @return RecipeDetail|null
     */
    public function findBySlug(string $slug): ?array
    {
        $stmt = $this-&gt;db-&gt;prepare(
            'SELECT r.*, u.name AS author_name
               FROM recipes r
               JOIN users u ON u.user_id = r.author_id
              WHERE r.slug = :slug
              LIMIT 1'
        );
        $stmt-&gt;execute([':slug' =&gt; $slug]);

        /** @var RecipeDetail|false $row */
        $row = $stmt-&gt;fetch(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);

        return $row === false ? null : $row;
    }

    public function incrementViews(int $id): void
    {
        $stmt = $this-&gt;db-&gt;prepare('UPDATE recipes SET views = views + 1 WHERE id = :id');
        $stmt-&gt;execute([':id' =&gt; $id]);
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>The <code>@phpstan-type</code> lines describe the exact shape of a row, so the rest of the code (and PHPStan) knows which keys exist. Every value that came from a visitor is bound as a parameter, never concatenated into the SQL.</p>

<div class="bg-yellow-100 border-l-4 border-yellow-500 text-yellow-700 p-4 my-4"><strong>Portability:</strong> <code>LIMIT :limit OFFSET :offset</code> works on MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQLite. SQL Server uses <code>ORDER BY ... OFFSET :offset ROWS FETCH NEXT :limit ROWS ONLY</code> instead. If you target SQL Server, swap that clause in the two paginated queries.</div>

<hr>

<h2 id="routes">Step 5: Named Routes</h2>
<p>Give every route a name and refer to it by name from then on. Links keep working if you ever change a URL, or install the site in a subdirectory.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">routes/web.php (add these lines)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

use App\Controllers\RecipeController;
use Rhapsody\Core\Routing\Router;

Router::get('/recipes', [RecipeController::class, 'index'])-&gt;name('recipes.index');

// Static paths go BEFORE the {slug} wildcard: the first matching route wins,
// so /recipes/new would otherwise be read as a recipe called "new".
Router::get('/recipes/new', [RecipeController::class, 'create'])
    -&gt;name('recipes.create')
    -&gt;middleware('auth');
Router::post('/recipes', [RecipeController::class, 'store'])
    -&gt;name('recipes.store')
    -&gt;middleware('auth');

Router::get('/recipes/{slug}', [RecipeController::class, 'show'])-&gt;name('recipes.show');
Router::post('/recipes/{id}/favorite', [RecipeController::class, 'favorite'])-&gt;name('recipes.favorite');</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li>The router passes the <code>Request</code> to every action first, then the route parameters in order: <code>show(Request $request, string $slug)</code>.</li>
    <li><code>->middleware('auth')</code> uses the key from <code>config/config.php</code>. Visitors who are not logged in are sent to <code>/login</code> before your controller runs.</li>
    <li>CSRF protection is global middleware, so every <code>POST</code> here is checked automatically. You only have to send the token (Steps 7 and 11).</li>
    <li>If you use the route cache, run <code>php rhapsody route:clear</code> after adding routes, or the new URLs will 404.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2 id="controller">Step 6: The Controller</h2>
<p>Generate the file, then replace its contents with the code below. The class is built in six parts; they all belong in the same file, in this order.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody make:controller RecipeController</code></pre>

<h3>6.1 Dependencies</h3>
<p>Type-hint what you need in the constructor and the container supplies it. <code>BaseController</code> already gives you <code>$this->twig</code>, <code>$this->cache</code> and <code>$this->schema</code>, so only add what is specific to this controller.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Controllers/RecipeController.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Controllers;

use App\Entities\Recipe;
use App\Events\RecipePublished;
use App\Jobs\EmailRecipeConfirmation;
use App\Models\RecipeCatalog;
use App\Services\RecipeExporter;
use Doctrine\ORM\EntityManager;
use Rhapsody\Core\BaseController;
use Rhapsody\Core\Events\EventDispatcher;
use Rhapsody\Core\Exceptions\HttpException;
use Rhapsody\Core\FileUploader;
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\ImageSigner;
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;
use Rhapsody\Core\Pagination;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\QueueManager;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;
use Rhapsody\Core\Routing\Router;
use Rhapsody\Core\SEO\Sluggify;
use Rhapsody\Core\Session;
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Config;
use Rhapsody\Core\Validator;
use Twig\Environment;

/**
 * @phpstan-import-type RecipeRow from RecipeCatalog
 */
final class RecipeController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(
        Environment $twig,
        private readonly EntityManager $em,
        private readonly RecipeCatalog $catalog,
        private readonly RecipeExporter $exporter,
        private readonly Validator $validator,
        private readonly EventDispatcher $events,
        private readonly QueueManager $queue,
        private readonly Config $config,
    ) {
        parent::__construct($twig);
    }</code></pre>

<h3>6.2 The list page</h3>
<p>This action combines pagination, typed configuration, the cache, and per-page SEO metadata. <code>Request::int()</code> always returns an integer, so a visitor typing <code>?page=abc</code> simply gets page 1.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Controllers/RecipeController.php (continued)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">    public function index(Request $request): Response
    {
        $pagination = new Pagination(
            $this-&gt;catalog-&gt;countAll(),
            $this-&gt;config-&gt;int('recipes.per_page', 9),
            max(1, $request-&gt;int('page', 1)),
        );

        $recipes = array_map(
            $this-&gt;withCardImage(...),
            $this-&gt;catalog-&gt;findPaginated($pagination-&gt;getLimit(), $pagination-&gt;getOffset()),
        );

        return $this-&gt;view('recipes/index.twig', [
            'recipes'    =&gt; $recipes,
            'pagination' =&gt; $pagination,
            'popular'    =&gt; $this-&gt;popular(),
            'recent'     =&gt; $this-&gt;recentRecipes(),
        ], [
            'title'         =&gt; 'Recipe Box: home cooking shared by the community',
            'description'   =&gt; 'Browse home-cooked recipes with photos and ingredient lists that scale to any number of servings.',
            'canonical_url' =&gt; $request-&gt;getCanonicalUrl(),
        ]);
    }</code></pre>
<p>The third argument to <code>view()</code> becomes the page's <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, meta description and canonical link (see <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/seo">SEO Helpers</a>).</p>

<h3>6.3 The recipe page</h3>
<p>Three things happen here that make the page fast and findable: the view counter is <code>defer()</code>red so the visitor never waits for the database write, the visit is remembered in the <strong>session</strong>, and a <strong>Schema.org</strong> <code>Recipe</code> block is added so search engines can show rich results.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Controllers/RecipeController.php (continued)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">    public function show(Request $request, string $slug): Response
    {
        $recipe = $this-&gt;catalog-&gt;findBySlug($slug);

        if ($recipe === null) {
            throw new HttpException(404, 'That recipe does not exist.');
        }

        $id = (int) $recipe['id'];

        // Counting a view is not worth making the visitor wait: run it after the response.
        defer(fn () =&gt; $this-&gt;catalog-&gt;incrementViews($id));

        $this-&gt;rememberVisit($recipe['slug'], $recipe['title']);

        $ingredients = $this-&gt;lines($recipe['ingredients']);
        $steps       = $this-&gt;lines($recipe['steps']);
        $photo       = $recipe['photo'] === null ? null : $this-&gt;imageSet($recipe['photo'], 960, 640);

        $this-&gt;schema-&gt;add('Recipe', array_filter([
            'name'               =&gt; $recipe['title'],
            'description'        =&gt; $recipe['summary'],
            'image'              =&gt; $photo['src'] ?? null,
            'author'             =&gt; ['@type' =&gt; 'Person', 'name' =&gt; $recipe['author_name']],
            'datePublished'      =&gt; substr($recipe['created_at'], 0, 10),
            'recipeYield'        =&gt; $recipe['servings'] . ' servings',
            'recipeIngredient'   =&gt; $ingredients,
            'recipeInstructions' =&gt; array_map(
                static fn (string $step): array =&gt; ['@type' =&gt; 'HowToStep', 'text' =&gt; $step],
                $steps,
            ),
        ], static fn (mixed $value): bool =&gt; $value !== null));

        return $this-&gt;view('recipes/show.twig', [
            'recipe'      =&gt; $recipe,
            'ingredients' =&gt; $ingredients,
            'steps'       =&gt; $steps,
            'photo'       =&gt; $photo,
            'favorite'    =&gt; in_array($id, $this-&gt;favoriteIds(), true),
        ], [
            'title'         =&gt; $recipe['title'] . ' | Recipe Box',
            'description'   =&gt; $recipe['summary'],
            'canonical_url' =&gt; $request-&gt;getCanonicalUrl(),
        ]);
    }</code></pre>
<p>Throwing <code>HttpException</code> hands the request to the framework's error handler, so a missing recipe renders your theme's <code>errors/404.twig</code> page.</p>

<h3>6.4 Sharing a recipe</h3>
<p>This is the heart of the tutorial. Read it top to bottom: validate, upload, save, announce, defer, queue, redirect.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Controllers/RecipeController.php (continued)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">    public function create(): Response
    {
        return $this-&gt;view('recipes/create.twig', ['old' =&gt; [], 'errors' =&gt; []], [
            'title' =&gt; 'Share a recipe | Recipe Box',
        ]);
    }

    public function store(Request $request): Response
    {
        // 1. Validate. Typed accessors never throw; they turn bad input into a safe default.
        $input = [
            'title'       =&gt; trim($request-&gt;string('title')),
            'summary'     =&gt; trim($request-&gt;string('summary')),
            'ingredients' =&gt; trim($request-&gt;string('ingredients')),
            'steps'       =&gt; trim($request-&gt;string('steps')),
            'servings'    =&gt; $request-&gt;int('servings'),
        ];

        $valid = $this-&gt;validator-&gt;validate($input, [
            'title'       =&gt; 'required|min:3|max:160',
            'summary'     =&gt; 'required|max:300',
            'ingredients' =&gt; 'required|min:10',
            'steps'       =&gt; 'required|min:10',
            'servings'    =&gt; 'required|numeric|min:1|max:50',
        ]);
        $errors = $this-&gt;validator-&gt;getErrors();

        // 2. Store the photo (optional). Only touch the disk once the text is valid.
        $upload = null;

        if ($valid &amp;&amp; $this-&gt;hasPhoto($request)) {
            $upload = new FileUploader($request)
                -&gt;images()
                -&gt;maxSize('4MB')
                -&gt;to(Path::public('images/recipes'))
                -&gt;store('photo');

            if ($upload-&gt;failed()) {
                $valid           = false;
                $errors['photo'] = [$upload-&gt;firstError() ?? 'The photo could not be uploaded.'];
            }
        }

        if (! $valid) {
            return $this-&gt;view('recipes/create.twig', ['old' =&gt; $input, 'errors' =&gt; $errors])
                -&gt;setStatusCode(422);
        }

        // 3. Save with Doctrine.
        $authorId = Session::string('user_id');
        $slug     = $this-&gt;uniqueSlug($input['title']);

        $recipe = new Recipe(
            authorId: $authorId,
            title: $input['title'],
            slug: $slug,
            summary: $input['summary'],
            ingredients: $input['ingredients'],
            steps: $input['steps'],
            servings: $input['servings'],
            photo: $upload?-&gt;first()?-&gt;filename,
        );

        $this-&gt;em-&gt;persist($recipe);
        $this-&gt;em-&gt;flush();

        $id = $recipe-&gt;getId() ?? throw new \LogicException('Doctrine did not assign an id.');

        // 4. Announce it. Listeners react without this controller knowing they exist.
        $this-&gt;events-&gt;dispatch(new RecipePublished($id, $slug, $authorId));

        // 5. Quick, best-effort work after the response: defer().
        if ($this-&gt;config-&gt;bool('recipes.export_enabled', true)) {
            defer($this-&gt;exporter-&gt;export(...));
        }

        // 6. Work that must not be lost: the persistent queue.
        $this-&gt;queue-&gt;dispatch(new EmailRecipeConfirmation($id), queue: 'emails');

        // 7. Flash message + redirect to the new page, using its route name.
        return redirect(Router::generateUrl('recipes.show', ['slug' =&gt; $slug]))
            -&gt;with('success', 'Your recipe is live!');
    }</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Validation errors</strong> come back as a list per field, which is what the template in Step 7 expects. The <code>422</code> status tells browsers and tools the submission was understood but rejected.</li>
    <li><strong>The uploader</strong> checks the file's real type from its contents, never the name the browser sent, renames it to a random name, and rejects SVG because SVG can carry scripts. See <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/file-uploader">File Uploader</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>The flash message</strong> set by <code>with()</code> lives for exactly one more request, which is the page you redirect to.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6.5 The favorite button's endpoint</h3>
<p>One route serves two kinds of client. A React island asks for JSON; a plain form post (or a visitor with JavaScript disabled) gets a redirect. <code>Request::wantsJson()</code> is how the action tells them apart.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Controllers/RecipeController.php (continued)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">    public function favorite(Request $request, string $id): Response
    {
        $recipeId = (int) $id;

        if ($this-&gt;em-&gt;find(Recipe::class, $recipeId) === null) {
            throw new HttpException(404, 'That recipe does not exist.');
        }

        $favorites  = $this-&gt;favoriteIds();
        $isFavorite = in_array($recipeId, $favorites, true);

        $favorites = $isFavorite
            ? array_values(array_diff($favorites, [$recipeId]))
            : [...$favorites, $recipeId];

        Session::set('favorites', $favorites);

        if ($request-&gt;wantsJson()) {
            return $this-&gt;json(['favorite' =&gt; ! $isFavorite, 'count' =&gt; count($favorites)]);
        }

        return redirect(Router::generateUrl('recipes.index'))
            -&gt;with('success', $isFavorite ? 'Removed from your favorites.' : 'Saved to your favorites.');
    }</code></pre>

<h3>6.6 Private helpers</h3>
<p>Small private methods keep the actions readable. Notice how much of the framework shows up here in a few lines: the cache, the session, signed image URLs and the slug helper.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Controllers/RecipeController.php (continued)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">    /**
     * The five most-viewed recipes, cached for ten minutes. The cache stores JSON,
     * so we cache plain arrays (not objects).
     *
     * @return list&lt;RecipeRow&gt;
     */
    private function popular(): array
    {
        /** @var list&lt;RecipeRow&gt; $rows */
        $rows = $this-&gt;cache-&gt;remember('recipes.popular', 10, fn (): array =&gt; $this-&gt;catalog-&gt;popular(5));

        return $rows;
    }

    /**
     * Recipe ids this visitor has saved, kept in their session.
     *
     * @return list&lt;int&gt;
     */
    private function favoriteIds(): array
    {
        $stored = Session::get('favorites', []);
        $ids    = [];

        if (is_array($stored)) {
            foreach ($stored as $value) {
                if (is_int($value)) {
                    $ids[] = $value;
                }
            }
        }

        return $ids;
    }

    /**
     * Newest first, at most five: slug =&gt; title.
     *
     * @return array&lt;string, string&gt;
     */
    private function recentRecipes(): array
    {
        $stored = Session::get('recent_recipes', []);
        $recent = [];

        if (is_array($stored)) {
            foreach ($stored as $slug =&gt; $title) {
                if (is_string($title)) {
                    $recent[(string) $slug] = $title;
                }
            }
        }

        return $recent;
    }

    private function rememberVisit(string $slug, string $title): void
    {
        $recent = $this-&gt;recentRecipes();
        unset($recent[$slug]);

        Session::set('recent_recipes', array_slice([$slug =&gt; $title] + $recent, 0, 5, true));
    }

    /**
     * Resized, cropped WebP URLs for 1x and 2x screens. Every URL is signed, so
     * visitors cannot ask the server for arbitrary sizes.
     *
     * @return array{src: string, srcset: string}
     */
    private function imageSet(string $file, int $width, int $height): array
    {
        $variant = static fn (int $factor): string =&gt; ImageSigner::url('recipes/' . $file, [
            'w'   =&gt; $width * $factor,
            'h'   =&gt; $height * $factor,
            'fit' =&gt; 'crop',
            'fm'  =&gt; 'webp',
            'q'   =&gt; 80,
        ]);

        return [
            'src'    =&gt; $variant(1),
            'srcset' =&gt; $variant(1) . ' ' . $width . 'w, ' . $variant(2) . ' ' . ($width * 2) . 'w',
        ];
    }

    /**
     * Swaps a row's photo filename for its image URLs, ready for the template.
     *
     * @param  RecipeRow            $row
     * @return array&lt;string, mixed&gt;
     */
    private function withCardImage(array $row): array
    {
        return array_merge($row, [
            'photo' =&gt; $row['photo'] === null ? null : $this-&gt;imageSet($row['photo'], 480, 320),
        ]);
    }

    /**
     * Turns a textarea into a clean list: one entry per non-empty line.
     *
     * @return list&lt;string&gt;
     */
    private function lines(string $text): array
    {
        $lines = preg_split('/\R/', $text) ?: [];

        return array_values(array_filter(
            array_map('trim', $lines),
            static fn (string $line): bool =&gt; $line !== '',
        ));
    }

    private function uniqueSlug(string $title): string
    {
        $base = Sluggify::transform($title);
        $slug = $base;

        for ($suffix = 2; $this-&gt;em-&gt;getRepository(Recipe::class)-&gt;findOneBy(['slug' =&gt; $slug]) !== null; $suffix++) {
            $slug = $base . '-' . $suffix;
        }

        return $slug;
    }

    private function hasPhoto(Request $request): bool
    {
        $photo = $request-&gt;getFiles()['photo'] ?? null;

        return is_array($photo) &amp;&amp; ($photo['error'] ?? UPLOAD_ERR_NO_FILE) !== UPLOAD_ERR_NO_FILE;
    }
}</code></pre>

<div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4 my-4"><strong>Why images are signed:</strong> every distinct set of resize options creates a new cached file. Signing means only sizes your own code asked for can be generated. That is also why the URLs are built here in PHP and handed to the template ready to use: a URL assembled in JavaScript could not be signed.</div>

