<p>Browsers never re-fill a file input after a failed submit, so a visitor who hits a validation error has to choose the photo again. That is normal browser behaviour. For multi-file uploads with live progress bars, see the bundled <code>rhapsody-uploader.js</code> in the <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/file-uploader">File Uploader</a> docs.</p>

<hr>

<h2 id="events">Step 8: Events and Listeners</h2>
<p>When a recipe is published, several things should happen: caches go stale, an activity line is logged, and one day maybe a newsletter is queued. The controller should not know about any of that. It announces <em>what happened</em>, and listeners decide <em>what to do about it</em>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody make:event RecipePublished
php rhapsody make:listener ClearRecipeCaches
php rhapsody make:listener LogRecipeActivity</code></pre>

<h3>The event</h3>
<p>An event is a small data object. Fill in the constructor generated for you with the facts listeners will need:</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Events/RecipePublished.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Events;

use Rhapsody\Core\Events\Event;

final class RecipePublished extends Event
{
    public function __construct(
        public readonly int $recipeId,
        public readonly string $slug,
        public readonly string $authorId,
    ) {
    }
}</code></pre>

<h3>The listeners</h3>
<p>A listener is a class with a <code>handle()</code> method. The container builds it, so anything it type-hints in its constructor (the cache, the mailer, the entity manager) is injected for you.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Listeners/ClearRecipeCaches.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Listeners;

use App\Events\RecipePublished;
use Rhapsody\Core\Cache;

final class ClearRecipeCaches
{
    public function __construct(private readonly Cache $cache)
    {
    }

    public function handle(RecipePublished $event): void
    {
        $this-&gt;cache-&gt;forget('recipes.popular');
    }
}</code></pre>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Listeners/LogRecipeActivity.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Listeners;

use App\Events\RecipePublished;
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;
use Rhapsody\Core\Logger;

final class LogRecipeActivity
{
    public function handle(RecipePublished $event): void
    {
        new Logger(Path::logs('recipes.log'))-&gt;log(
            "Recipe #{$event-&gt;recipeId} ({$event-&gt;slug}) published by {$event-&gt;authorId}",
        );
    }
}</code></pre>

<h3>Register them</h3>
<p>Map the event to its listeners in the provider. They run in the order listed.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Providers/EventServiceProvider.php (add to the $listen array, with the matching use lines)</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">RecipePublished::class =&gt; [
    ClearRecipeCaches::class,
    LogRecipeActivity::class,
],</code></pre>

<div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4 my-4"><strong>Tip:</strong> You can also attach a listener in code, for example inside a service: <code>$dispatcher->listen(RecipePublished::class, fn (RecipePublished $event) => ...);</code>. Listeners are matched by the <strong>exact</strong> event class.</div>
<div class="bg-yellow-100 border-l-4 border-yellow-500 text-yellow-700 p-4 my-4"><strong>Warning:</strong> A listener that throws is logged and skipped; it never breaks the request. That makes listeners the right place for side effects (logging, cache clearing, notifications) and the wrong place for anything the user's action <em>depends</em> on, such as saving the recipe itself. That belongs in the controller, as in Step 6.4.</div>

<hr>

<h2 id="defer">Step 9: Work After the Response with defer()</h2>
<p>Some jobs are quick and nice-to-have: counting a view, refreshing a file. <code>defer()</code> runs them right after the visitor has their page. You have already used it twice in the controller. Here is the service behind the second one:</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Services/RecipeExporter.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Services;

use App\Models\RecipeCatalog;
use Rhapsody\Core\Helpers\Path;
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Fs;
use Rhapsody\Core\Support\Json;

final readonly class RecipeExporter
{
    public function __construct(private RecipeCatalog $catalog)
    {
    }

    /**
     * Writes the newest recipes to storage/exports/recipes.json.
     */
    public function export(): void
    {
        $directory = Path::storage('exports');

        if (! is_dir($directory) &amp;&amp; ! mkdir($directory, 0755, true) &amp;&amp; ! is_dir($directory)) {
            throw new \RuntimeException('Unable to create the export directory.');
        }

        Fs::write(
            Path::storage('exports/recipes.json'),
            Json::encode($this-&gt;catalog-&gt;findPaginated(500, 0), JSON_PRETTY_PRINT),
            LOCK_EX,
        );
    }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li><code>Path::storage()</code> builds a reliable absolute path that works from a web request, a CLI command or a worker alike.</li>
    <li><code>Fs::write()</code> and <code>Json::encode()</code> throw a clear exception on failure instead of returning <code>false</code>. Inside a deferred task that exception is written to <code>storage/logs/defer.log</code> and never reaches the visitor.</li>
    <li><code>$this->exporter->export(...)</code> is a first-class callable, so <code>defer()</code> receives the method directly with nothing to wrap.</li>
</ul>
<div class="bg-yellow-100 border-l-4 border-yellow-500 text-yellow-700 p-4 my-4"><strong>Warning:</strong> Deferred tasks cannot change the response and cannot write to the session (it is already closed), and they are lost if the server stops while they wait. Keep them small, and capture plain values such as an id.</div>

<hr>

<h2 id="queue">Step 10: Work That Must Not Be Lost: the Queue</h2>
<p>A confirmation email is different from a view counter: if the mail server hiccups, you want a retry, not silence. A queued job is stored in the database first, then run by a worker process, with automatic retries.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-mono text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">app/Jobs/EmailRecipeConfirmation.php</p>
<pre><code class="language-php">&lt;?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Jobs;

use App\Entities\Recipe;
use App\Entities\User;
use Doctrine\ORM\EntityManager;
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\JobInterface;
use Rhapsody\Core\Mailer;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\Attributes\Backoff;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\Attributes\Timeout;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\Attributes\Tries;

#[Tries(5)]
#[Backoff(30)]
#[Timeout(45)]
final class EmailRecipeConfirmation implements JobInterface
{
    public function __construct(public readonly int $recipeId)
    {
    }

    public function handle(EntityManager $em, Mailer $mailer): void
    {
        $recipe = $em-&gt;find(Recipe::class, $this-&gt;recipeId);

        if ($recipe === null) {
            return; // Deleted before the worker got to it: nothing left to do.
        }

        $author = $em-&gt;find(User::class, $recipe-&gt;getAuthorId());

        if ($author === null) {
            return;
        }

        $name  = htmlspecialchars($author-&gt;getName(), ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');
        $title = htmlspecialchars($recipe-&gt;getTitle(), ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');

        $mailer-&gt;send(
            $author-&gt;getEmail(),
            'Your recipe is live: ' . $recipe-&gt;getTitle(),
            "&lt;p&gt;Hi {$name},&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;{$title}&amp;rdquo; has been published on Recipe Box.&lt;/p&gt;",
        );
    }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The constructor holds only an id.</strong> The job stores plain values, then loads fresh data inside <code>handle()</code>. That also avoids emailing about a recipe that has since been deleted.</li>
    <li><strong><code>handle()</code> gets its services from the container</strong>, exactly like a controller constructor.</li>
    <li><strong>The attributes</strong> tune retries: up to 5 attempts, waiting 30s, 60s, 120s... between them, and no single attempt may run longer than 45 seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>A job may run twice</strong> if a worker dies at the wrong moment, so write <code>handle()</code> to be safe to repeat.</li>
</ul>
<p>The controller already dispatches it, onto a queue named <code>emails</code>. Start a worker to process the queue (see the <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/queue">Persistent Queue</a> page for running it under Supervisor or from cron):</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody queue:work --queue=emails,default</code></pre>

<div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="min-w-full text-left border border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
            <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Tool</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Runs</th>
